//!
//! # Processes
//!
//! User processes: an address space, the threads that run in it, and the
//! bookkeeping that outlives them.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! A process is a slot in a fixed table. It owns the address space, the
//! regions, the descriptors, the current directory, the parent and the exit
//! status; its threads own their registers, kernel stacks and states. The
//! slot counts its threads (`threadCount`), and each thread records its
//! slot (`Sched::Thread::Control::process`, set by
//! `Sched::Scheduler::spawnMember`), which is how system calls find the
//! calling process. `launch` makes the first thread; `addThread` adds one
//! to a running process. A thread starts in `userMain`, which drops to
//! ring 3 and never comes back: from then on the thread is only the kernel
//! stack that interrupts and system calls of the process run on.
//!
//! The process ends when its last thread ends. That thread waits until its
//! siblings are reaped (`Sched::Scheduler::awaitSharers`), closes the
//! descriptors, ends the regions, records the state and ends; its reap, the
//! last one holding the space, destroys the address space. `wait`
//! collects the status and frees the slot.
//!
//! ```text
//!   exitThread(n)                  one thread goes
//!     └─ finishThread: status := n unless fixed, threadCount -= 1
//!          ├─ others remain: leave the process, Sched::Scheduler::exitCurrent
//!          └─ last one: awaitSharers, closeAll, teardown, reset,
//!                       state := EXITED, thread := this one, exitCurrent
//!   exitCurrent(n), killCurrent(n), kill(id)    the whole process goes
//!     └─ requestEnd: status := n once (the first event wins), killPending,
//!                    interrupt the other threads, cancel their reads
//!          └─ each thread: finishThread at its next checkKill
//! ```
//!
//! The process id is not the scheduler's thread id. The Linux thread id
//! (`Sched::Thread::Control::tid`, `gettid`) comes from the process id
//! counter: the first thread's is the process id, every later thread's
//! (`addThread`, `reserveThreadId`) is an id no process will get.
//!
//! ## Lifetime
//!
//! ```text
//!   Proc::Programs::run("hello one two")
//!     └─ Proc::Process::startElf(file node, arguments)
//!          ├─ Mm::AddressSpace::create            new PML4, kernel half shared
//!          ├─ Proc::Elf::load                       stage one image region per PT_LOAD
//!          │                                  (node counts, reservation, tables;
//!          │                                  no page mapped: they come from the
//!          │                                  page cache on first access)
//!          ├─ mapStack                        stack tables, 2 zeroed pages
//!          ├─ buildStack                      argc, argv, envp, auxv, strings
//!          └─ launch
//!               ├─ slot := RUNNING, id := nextId++
//!               ├─ Proc::Memory::reset, then adoptImage   the slot gets the regions
//!               └─ Sched::Scheduler::spawnMember(userMain, slot, root, slot + 1)
//!                                                   the process owns `root`
//!
//!   any failure before the thread exists: Proc::Memory::dropStaged gives the
//!   node counts and reservations back, once, and the space is destroyed
//!
//!   the new thread:  userMain ─► Arch::UserMode::enter ─► ring 3 ...
//!                                    ... syscall exit(n) or a fault ...
//!   finishThread (last thread): state := EXITED, print "process N exited"
//!                      └─ Sched::Scheduler::exitCurrent   thread DEAD
//!   next thread:     reapZombie frees the kernel stack and, no other
//!                    thread holding it, destroys the address space
//!                    (every frame goes back to the PMM)
//!   Proc::Process::wait:   joins the threads of the process until it is
//!                    EXITED and its last thread gone, returns the status,
//!                    slot := FREE
//! ```
//!
//! ## Parents and children
//!
//! A process remembers the process that started it (`parent`; `NO_PARENT` for
//! one the kernel started, which the kernel waits for itself). Only the
//! parent may wait for a process. When a process ends, `releaseChildren`
//! frees its children that already ended, since nobody can wait for them
//! any more, and makes the running ones orphans (`detached`): an orphan's
//! slot is freed as soon as it ends. So no slot stays taken by a process
//! nobody can collect.
//!
//! ## Killing
//!
//! `kill` records `KILLED` (137) as the status unless an earlier end set
//! one, marks the process (`killPending`) and wakes every thread of it
//! that waits in a sleep, a wait or a keyboard read. A fatal fault and
//! `exit_group` do the same from inside the process. Each thread ends the
//! next time it would return to ring 3: `checkKill` runs at the end of
//! every system call and of every interrupt taken from ring 3. A thread is
//! never ended in the middle of kernel code, so no lock or half-done change
//! is left behind.
//!
//! ## User Address Space of a Process
//!
//! ```text
//!   0x0000000000400000  program segments (text r-x, rodata r--, data rw-):
//!                       image regions, read-only pages shared through the
//!                       page cache, data pages copied on first write
//!   0x00007FFFFF7FF000  ┐ STACK_BOTTOM: the stack may grow down to here
//!                       │ stack: up to 8 MiB, read/write, no-execute; the
//!                       │ top 2 pages are mapped at start, the rest on
//!                       │ first access (`Proc::Memory::growStack`)
//!   0x00007FFFFFFFF000  ┘ STACK_TOP = Mm::AddressSpace::USER_LIMIT, the first
//!                         byte past the stack and the page that stays unmapped
//!   an ELF program starts with the Linux initial stack at the top of it
//!   (argc, argv, an empty envp, the auxiliary vector, the argument strings;
//!   see `buildStack`) and RSP 16-byte aligned on argc. A raw test program
//!   from `startCode` gets RSP = STACK_TOP - 8 instead, as if a `call` had
//!   just pushed a return address.
//! ```
//!
//! See concept doc 14.

import Arch from "@arch";
import Boot from "@boot";
import Drivers from "@drivers";
import Fs from "@fs";
import Lib from "@lib";
import Mm from "@mm";
import Sched from "@sched";
import _ from "./elf";
import _ from "./memory";

/// `Proc::Futex::clearChildTid`, by its symbol: `futex.ign` reads user
/// memory through `Proc::User`, which imports this file, so this file
/// cannot import it back.
extern FutexHook {
  /// Writes a 32-bit 0 at `address` in `root` and wakes one waiter on it.
  function ignis_futex_clear_child_tid(root: u64, address: u64): void;
}

/// One entry of the process table.
@cLayout
record ProcessEntry {
  /// The process id, from a counter that starts at 1 and never repeats.
  public id: u64;

  /// `FREE`, `RUNNING` or `EXITED`.
  public state: u64;

  /// Id of the first thread while the process runs; once `EXITED`, the id
  /// of its last thread, whose reap destroys the address space.
  public thread: u64;

  /// User address the program starts at.
  public entry: u64;

  /// User stack pointer the program starts with.
  public stackPointer: u64;

  /// The exit status, valid once the state is `EXITED`.
  public status: u64;

  /// The system call convention of the process, `ABI_LINUX` or `ABI_NATIVE`.
  public abi: u64;

  /// The id of the process that started this one, `NO_PARENT` for a process
  /// the kernel started (the kernel waits for those itself).
  public parent: u64;

  /// True for an orphan: its parent ended first, nobody can wait for it,
  /// and its slot is freed as soon as it ends.
  public detached: boolean;

  /// True once the process is ending (`kill`, a fatal fault, `exit_group`):
  /// each thread ends the next time it leaves the kernel (`checkKill`),
  /// and no thread is added.
  public killPending: boolean;

  /// The address space, destroyed after the last thread is reaped.
  public root: u64;

  /// The threads that have not ended yet.
  public threadCount: u64;

  /// True once an end of the whole process recorded `status`; a later end
  /// or thread exit does not change it.
  public statusFixed: boolean;

  /// True when that end was a kill or a fault, for the console report.
  public killedEnd: boolean;
}

/// The process table and the id counter.
record ProcessState {
  /// The process slots.
  static mut table: ProcessEntry[64];

  /// The id the next process gets.
  static mut nextId: u64;

  /// Guards every slot's state, id, parent, detached flag and status, and
  /// `nextId`, every thread count, root and end field, and the process
  /// slot each thread records; rank `Lib::MutexRank::PROCESS_TABLE`. A
  /// process's own `killPending`, `abi`, entry and stack pointer are read
  /// without it.
  static mut lock: Sched::Mutex;
}

/// The process API.
namespace Proc::Process {
  /// Size of the process table.
  const MAX_PROCESSES: u64 = 64;

  /// Returned instead of a process id or a slot when there is none.
  const NO_PROCESS: u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;

  /// Slot state: not in use.
  const FREE: u64 = 0;

  /// Slot state: the process was started and has not ended.
  const RUNNING: u64 = 1;

  /// Slot state: the process ended and its status waits for `wait`.
  const EXITED: u64 = 2;

  /// `ProcessEntry::parent` of a process the kernel started.
  const NO_PARENT: u64 = 0;

  /// The exit status of a killed process: 128 + 9, as a shell shows SIGKILL.
  const KILLED: u64 = 137;

  /// `kill` worked.
  const KILL_OK: u64 = 0;

  /// `kill` found no process with that id.
  const KILL_NOT_FOUND: u64 = 1;

  /// The system call convention the process speaks: Linux x86_64 numbers and
  /// errno values.
  const ABI_LINUX: u64 = 0;

  /// The native IgniOS system call ABI (`Syscall::Native`): own numbers,
  /// status in RAX and value in RDX. Chosen by the IgniOS note of the ELF.
  const ABI_NATIVE: u64 = 1;

  /// 4 KiB.
  const PAGE_SIZE: u64 = 4096;

  /// Where raw test programs are loaded.
  const CODE_BASE: u64 = 0x400000;

  /// The stack pages mapped when a process starts: the arguments fit in
  /// them (`buildStack`). The rest of the stack, down to
  /// `Proc::Memory::STACK_BOTTOM`, is mapped on first access.
  const INITIAL_STACK_PAGES: u64 = 2;

  /// The first address past the user stack (`Proc::Memory::STACK_TOP`).
  const STACK_TOP: u64 = Proc::Memory::STACK_TOP;

  /// The most arguments a program gets, its name included.
  const MAX_ARGUMENTS: u64 = 32;

  /// The most bytes the argument strings take, their NULs included. The
  /// strings and the argv array then fit the first two stack pages.
  const MAX_ARGUMENT_BYTES: u64 = 4096;

  /// Auxiliary vector type that ends the vector, `AT_NULL`.
  const AUX_NULL: u64 = 0;

  /// Auxiliary vector type of the page size, `AT_PAGESZ`.
  const AUX_PAGE_SIZE: u64 = 6;

  /// Auxiliary vector type of the program entry point, `AT_ENTRY`.
  const AUX_ENTRY: u64 = 9;

  /// Words of the initial stack that do not depend on the argument count:
  /// argc, the 0 after argv, the 0 that is the empty envp, and three
  /// auxiliary vector pairs.
  const FIXED_STACK_WORDS: u64 = 9;

  /// Process ids start at 1.
  function initialize(): void {
    ProcessState::nextId = 1;
    Sched::Mutex::initialize(tableLock(), Lib::MutexRank::PROCESS_TABLE);
    Proc::Memory::initialize();
  }

  /// Starts a process whose whole image is `length` bytes of machine code
  /// copied to `CODE_BASE`, read and execute, with a fresh stack. The code
  /// must fit one page. Returns the process id, or `NO_PROCESS`.
  function startCode(code: *u8, length: u64): u64 {
    if (length == 0 || length > PAGE_SIZE) {
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    let root: u64 = Mm::AddressSpace::create();

    if (root == Mm::Pmm::NO_FRAME) {
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    let frame: u64 = Mm::AddressSpace::allocatePage(root, CODE_BASE, 0);

    if (frame == Mm::Pmm::NO_FRAME || !mapStack(root)) {
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    Lib::Mem::copy(Boot::Hhdm::toVirtual(frame), code, length);

    return launch(root, CODE_BASE, STACK_TOP - 8, ABI_LINUX, Fs::Vfs::root(), null);
  }

  /// Starts a process from the ELF executable file `node` and a fresh stack
  /// that holds `count` arguments. The caller keeps `node` from being
  /// removed or written until this returns (`Fs::Vfs::pin`); the process's
  /// image regions hold it afterwards. The VFS lock is not held: the
  /// process table lock ranks below it. `strings` is the
  /// `stringBytes` bytes of the arguments, each one ended by a NUL, in order.
  /// The process starts in the current directory `directory`.
  /// Prints what the loader mapped, or why it refused the file. Returns the
  /// process id, or `NO_PROCESS`.
  function startElf(
    node: *mut Fs::Node,
    strings: *u8,
    stringBytes: u64,
    count: u64,
    directory: *mut Fs::Node
  ): u64 {
    if (count == 0 || count > MAX_ARGUMENTS || stringBytes > MAX_ARGUMENT_BYTES) {
      Drivers::Console::writeLine("elf: too many arguments");
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    let root: u64 = Mm::AddressSpace::create();

    if (root == Mm::Pmm::NO_FRAME) {
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    let mut loaded: Proc::LoadedImage = Proc::LoadedImage { entry: 0, segments: 0, pages: 0, abi: 0 };
    let mut staged: Proc::StagedImage = Proc::StagedImage { table: 0, count: 0 };
    let image: *mut Proc::StagedImage = &mut staged as *mut Proc::StagedImage;
    let error: u64 = Proc::Elf::load(node, root, image, &mut loaded as *mut Proc::LoadedImage);

    if (error != Proc::Elf::OK) {
      Drivers::Console::write("elf: ");
      Drivers::Console::writeLine(Proc::Elf::describe(error));
      Proc::Memory::dropStaged(image);
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    if (!mapStack(root)) {
      Drivers::Console::writeLine("elf: out of memory for the stack");
      Proc::Memory::dropStaged(image);
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    Drivers::Console::write("elf: ");
    Drivers::Console::writeDecimal(loaded.segments);
    Drivers::Console::write(" segments, ");
    Drivers::Console::writeDecimal(loaded.pages);
    Drivers::Console::write(" pages, entry ");
    Drivers::Console::writeHex(loaded.entry);
    let stackPointer: u64 = buildStack(root, strings, stringBytes, count, loaded.entry);

    if (stackPointer == 0) {
      Drivers::Console::writeLine("elf: out of memory for the arguments");
      Proc::Memory::dropStaged(image);
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    Drivers::Console::write(", stack pointer ");
    Drivers::Console::writeHex(stackPointer);
    Drivers::Console::write(", argc ");
    Drivers::Console::writeDecimal(count);
    Drivers::Console::write(loaded.abi == ABI_NATIVE ? ", abi native" : ", abi linux");
    Drivers::Console::writeLine("");

    return launch(root, loaded.entry, stackPointer, loaded.abi, directory, image);
  }

  /// Writes the initial stack of a program to the stack pages of `root`, the
  /// layout Linux gives a static executable, and returns the stack pointer
  /// the program starts with, or 0 when memory runs out:
  ///
  /// ```text
  ///   STACK_TOP           ┐
  ///                       │ the argument strings, NUL-terminated
  ///   stringsStart        ┘ (STACK_TOP - stringBytes rounded up to 16)
  ///                         padding to a 16-byte boundary
  ///                         AT_NULL, 0
  ///                         AT_ENTRY, entry
  ///                         AT_PAGESZ, 4096          auxiliary vector
  ///                         0                        envp, empty
  ///                         0                        end of argv
  ///                         argv[count - 1] ... argv[0]   addresses in the strings
  ///   stack pointer ──►     argc                     16-byte aligned
  /// ```
  ///
  /// Unlike a function entry, nothing looks like a return address: the
  /// program's `_start` (`user/lib/linux/start.S`) reads argc at the stack
  /// pointer. `startElf` keeps the arguments small enough for the two top
  /// stack pages.
  function buildStack(root: u64, strings: *u8, stringBytes: u64, count: u64, entry: u64): u64 {
    let stringsStart: u64 = STACK_TOP - (stringBytes + 15) / 16 * 16;
    let words: u64 = FIXED_STACK_WORDS + count;
    let stackPointer: u64 = (stringsStart - words * 8) / 16 * 16;
    let table: *mut u64 = Mm::Heap::allocate(words * 8, 16) as *mut u64;

    if (table == null) {
      return 0;
    }

    let mut index: u64 = 0;
    let mut offset: u64 = 0;

    writeWord(table, 0, count);

    while (index < count) {
      writeWord(table, 1 + index, stringsStart + offset);

      while (*(strings + offset) != 0) {
        offset += 1;
      }

      offset += 1;
      index += 1;
    }

    writeWord(table, 1 + count, 0);
    writeWord(table, 2 + count, 0);
    writeWord(table, 3 + count, AUX_PAGE_SIZE);
    writeWord(table, 4 + count, PAGE_SIZE);
    writeWord(table, 5 + count, AUX_ENTRY);
    writeWord(table, 6 + count, entry);
    writeWord(table, 7 + count, AUX_NULL);
    writeWord(table, 8 + count, 0);

    let copied: boolean = Mm::AddressSpace::copyToUser(root, stringsStart, strings, stringBytes) && Mm::AddressSpace::copyToUser(root, stackPointer, table as *u8, words * 8);

    Mm::Heap::free(table as *mut u8);

    return copied ? stackPointer : 0;
  }

  /// Stores `value` as word `index` of `table`.
  function writeWord(table: *mut u64, index: u64, value: u64): void {
    *@pointerFromInteger<*mut u64>(@integerFromPointer(table) + index * 8) = value;
  }

  /// Builds the page tables of the whole stack range and maps its first
  /// pages, zeroed and no-execute. Returns false when memory runs out; the
  /// caller destroys the space.
  function mapStack(root: u64): boolean {
    if (!Proc::Memory::prepareStack(root)) {
      return false;
    }

    let mut page: u64 = 1;

    while (page <= INITIAL_STACK_PAGES) {
      let address: u64 = STACK_TOP - page * PAGE_SIZE;

      if (Mm::AddressSpace::allocatePage(root, address, Mm::Vmm::WRITABLE | Mm::Vmm::NO_EXECUTE) == Mm::Pmm::NO_FRAME) {
        return false;
      }

      page += 1;
    }

    return true;
  }

  /// Makes a process of the address space `root`, which the process now owns
  /// and which must already hold the program and its stack, and queues its
  /// thread. The image regions `staged` (null for a raw program) become the
  /// slot's regions after `Proc::Memory::reset`. The program starts at
  /// `entry` with `stackPointer` and speaks the system call ABI `abi`, in the
  /// current directory `directory`. Returns the process id, or
  /// `NO_PROCESS`; on failure the space is destroyed and the staged regions
  /// dropped. The staged node counts are given back under the table lock,
  /// which ranks below the VFS lock that takes.
  function launch(
    root: u64,
    entry: u64,
    stackPointer: u64,
    abi: u64,
    directory: *mut Fs::Node,
    staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage
  ): u64 {
    return launchThread(root, entry, stackPointer, abi, directory, staged, Arch::ContextAsm::ignis_function_address(userMain));
  }

  /// Makes a process of the empty address space `root` whose first thread
  /// runs the kernel function `body(slot)` in ring 0 instead of a
  /// program, for the self-tests of thread and process lifetime. The
  /// function must end through `exitThread`, `exitCurrent` or `checkKill`,
  /// never by returning. Returns the process id, or `NO_PROCESS`; on
  /// failure the space is destroyed.
  function startKernel(root: u64, body: u64): u64 {
    return launchThread(root, 0, 0, ABI_LINUX, Fs::Vfs::root(), null, body);
  }

  /// `launch` with the first thread running `threadEntry(slot)`.
  function launchThread(
    root: u64,
    entry: u64,
    stackPointer: u64,
    abi: u64,
    directory: *mut Fs::Node,
    staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage,
    threadEntry: u64
  ): u64 {
    let caller: u64 = currentId();

    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let slot: u64 = findFreeSlot();

    if (slot == NO_PROCESS) {
      dropImage(staged);
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

    (*process).id = ProcessState::nextId;
    (*process).state = RUNNING;
    (*process).entry = entry;
    (*process).stackPointer = stackPointer;
    (*process).status = 0;
    (*process).abi = abi;
    (*process).parent = caller == NO_PROCESS ? NO_PARENT : caller;
    (*process).detached = false;
    (*process).killPending = false;
    (*process).root = root;
    (*process).threadCount = 0;
    (*process).statusFixed = false;
    (*process).killedEnd = false;

    Proc::Memory::reset(slot);

    if (Fs::Files::openStandard(slot) != Fs::Vfs::OK) {
      (*process).state = FREE;
      dropImage(staged);
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    Fs::Files::setCurrentDirectory(slot, directory);

    if (staged != null && !Proc::Memory::adoptImage(slot, staged)) {
      Fs::Files::closeAll(slot);
      (*process).state = FREE;
      dropImage(staged);
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    // No image page was touched, so the staging alone still describes what
    // the regions hold.
    let thread: u64 = Sched::Scheduler::spawnUser(threadEntry, slot, root, slot + 1, ProcessState::nextId, 0);

    if (thread == Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD) {
      Fs::Files::closeAll(slot);
      Proc::Memory::reset(slot);
      dropImage(staged);
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
      (*process).state = FREE;
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    if (staged != null) {
      Proc::Memory::discardStaged(staged);
    }

    (*process).thread = thread;
    (*process).threadCount = 1;
    ProcessState::nextId += 1;

    let id: u64 = (*process).id;

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return id;
  }

  /// Adds a thread to the running process in `slot`: it runs
  /// `entry(argument)` (an address from
  /// `Arch::ContextAsm::ignis_function_address`) in the process's address
  /// space and counts as one of its threads until it ends through
  /// `exitThread` or the end of the process. Refused, with `NO_THREAD`,
  /// when the slot holds no running process, the process is ending, or no
  /// thread slot is free. Returns the thread id. Its Linux thread id comes
  /// from the process id counter and its FS base is 0.
  function addThread(slot: u64, entry: u64, argument: u64): u64 {
    return addThreadWith(slot, entry, argument, 0, 0);
  }

  /// `addThread` for a thread with the Linux thread id `tid` (from
  /// `reserveThreadId`; 0 takes the next one) and the FS base `fsBase`.
  function addThreadWith(slot: u64, entry: u64, argument: u64, tid: u64, fsBase: u64): u64 {
    if (slot >= MAX_PROCESSES) {
      return Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD;
    }

    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

    if ((*process).state != RUNNING || (*process).killPending || (*process).threadCount == 0) {
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      return Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD;
    }

    let mut threadId: u64 = tid;

    if (threadId == 0) {
      threadId = ProcessState::nextId;
      ProcessState::nextId += 1;
    }

    let thread: u64 = Sched::Scheduler::spawnUser(entry, argument, (*process).root, slot + 1, threadId, fsBase);

    if (thread != Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD) {
      (*process).threadCount += 1;
    }

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return thread;
  }

  /// Takes the next id of the process id counter for a thread that is
  /// about to be added (`addThreadWith`), so it can be written to user
  /// memory before the thread runs. An id taken for a thread that is never
  /// added is lost, like any other.
  function reserveThreadId(): u64 {
    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let id: u64 = ProcessState::nextId;

    ProcessState::nextId += 1;
    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return id;
  }

  /// `Proc::Memory::dropStaged` of `staged`, when there is one.
  function dropImage(staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage): void {
    if (staged != null) {
      Proc::Memory::dropStaged(staged);
    }
  }

  /// Waits until the process `id` has ended and its address space is freed,
  /// then returns its exit status and frees its slot. Returns
  /// `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` for an id that is not in the table, and for one
  /// whose status another waiter collected first.
  ///
  /// While the process runs, the caller joins one of its threads at a
  /// time (`Sched::Scheduler::join`), so it sleeps until a thread is gone
  /// and then looks again. Once the process is `EXITED` it joins the last
  /// thread, whose reap destroys the address space. The table lock is let
  /// go during a join, so the slot is checked again after it: the id must
  /// still be there, ended. A killed caller stops waiting.
  function wait(id: u64): u64 {
    while (true) {
      Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

      let slot: u64 = findSlot(id);

      if (slot == NO_PROCESS) {
        Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
        return NO_PROCESS;
      }

      let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

      if ((*process).state == EXITED) {
        let last: u64 = (*process).thread;

        Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
        Sched::Scheduler::join(last);
        Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

        if ((*process).state != EXITED || (*process).id != id) {
          Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
          return NO_PROCESS;
        }

        let status: u64 = (*process).status;

        (*process).state = FREE;
        Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

        return status;
      }

      // A running process has a thread in the table until it is EXITED:
      // the last one leaves it under this lock.
      let thread: u64 = Sched::Scheduler::threadOfProcess(slot + 1);

      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

      if (thread == Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD) {
        Sched::Scheduler::yield();
      } else {
        Sched::Scheduler::join(thread);
      }

      if (killRequested()) {
        return NO_PROCESS;
      }
    }

    return NO_PROCESS;
  }

  /// The id of the process the running thread belongs to, or `NO_PROCESS`
  /// when it is a kernel thread.
  function currentId(): u64 {
    let slot: u64 = currentSlot();

    if (slot == NO_PROCESS) {
      return NO_PROCESS;
    }

    return (*slotPointer(slot)).id;
  }

  /// The system call convention of the running process. Set at launch and
  /// never changed, so read without the table lock.
  function currentAbi(): u64 {
    let slot: u64 = currentSlot();

    if (slot == NO_PROCESS) {
      return ABI_LINUX;
    }

    return (*slotPointer(slot)).abi;
  }

  /// The address space of the running process. Zero for a kernel thread.
  function currentRoot(): u64 {
    if (currentSlot() == NO_PROCESS) {
      return 0;
    }

    return Arch::Cpu::readCr3();
  }

  /// Ends the whole running process with `status` (native `EXIT`, Linux
  /// `exit_group`) and never returns: the status is recorded
  /// unless an earlier end set one, every other thread is interrupted and
  /// ends at its next `checkKill`, and the calling thread ends now.
  function exitCurrent(status: u64): never {
    endCurrent(status, false);
  }

  /// Ends the running process because it did something the kernel does not
  /// allow, as `exitCurrent` does. `status` follows the shell convention,
  /// 128 plus the number of the signal Linux would have sent. Never
  /// returns.
  function killCurrent(status: u64): never {
    endCurrent(status, true);
  }

  /// Ends only the calling thread (Linux `exit`). Its status becomes the
  /// process's when it is the last thread and no end of the whole process
  /// set one first. Never returns.
  function exitThread(status: u64): never {
    finishThread(status);
  }

  /// `exitCurrent` and `killCurrent`.
  function endCurrent(status: u64, killed: boolean): never {
    let slot: u64 = currentSlot();

    if (slot == NO_PROCESS) {
      @panic("process: a kernel thread tried to end a process");
    }

    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());
    requestEnd(slot, status, killed);
    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    finishThread(status);
  }

  /// Starts the end of the process in `slot`: records `status` unless an
  /// earlier end did (the first one wins), sets `killPending`, and wakes
  /// every thread of the process but the caller that waits in a sleep, a
  /// wait or a keyboard read, so each reaches `checkKill`. The table lock
  /// is held.
  function requestEnd(slot: u64, status: u64, killed: boolean): void {
    let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

    if (!(*process).statusFixed) {
      (*process).status = status;
      (*process).statusFixed = true;
      (*process).killedEnd = killed;
    }

    // The flag goes first: the interrupt and the read cancel make the
    // threads look at it.
    (*process).killPending = true;

    // A process that ends itself with one thread has nobody to tell.
    if (currentSlot() != slot || (*process).threadCount > 1) {
      Sched::Scheduler::interruptProcess(slot + 1, Sched::Scheduler::currentId());
      Fs::Files::cancelRead(slot);
    }
  }

  /// Ends the calling thread. Its `status` becomes the process's unless an
  /// end of the whole process fixed one. When other threads remain, only
  /// this one leaves. The last one waits until its siblings are reaped,
  /// releases the descriptors and regions, makes the slot `EXITED` (or
  /// `FREE` for an orphan), reports the end and ends; the reap that follows
  /// destroys the address space.
  ///
  /// First of all, while the thread is still a member and the space is
  /// whole, a clear-child-tid address (`set_tid_address`,
  /// `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID`) gets a 32-bit 0 and one futex waiter on it is
  /// woken (C4 D4), so a sibling joining on the tid word returns. The last
  /// thread does it too: nobody else can wait on the word then, since
  /// waits are keyed by the space, but one path for every thread keeps the
  /// rule simple and the write costs one copy.
  function finishThread(status: u64): never {
    let slot: u64 = currentSlot();

    if (slot == NO_PROCESS) {
      @panic("process: a kernel thread tried to end a process");
    }

    let clearAddress: u64 = Sched::Scheduler::currentClearChildTid();

    if (clearAddress != 0) {
      Sched::Scheduler::setCurrentClearChildTid(0);
      FutexHook::ignis_futex_clear_child_tid(currentRoot(), clearAddress);
    }

    let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    if (!(*process).statusFixed) {
      (*process).status = status;
    }

    (*process).threadCount -= 1;

    if ((*process).threadCount > 0) {
      Sched::Scheduler::setProcess(0);
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      Sched::Scheduler::exitCurrent();
    }

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    // Siblings that already left may not be reaped yet; once they are, this
    // thread alone holds the space, and its own reap destroys it.
    Sched::Scheduler::awaitSharers();

    // The descriptors and regions go first: once the slot is EXITED, or FREE
    // for an orphan, another process may be launched into it.
    Fs::Files::closeAll(slot);
    Proc::Memory::teardown(slot, currentRoot());
    Proc::Memory::reset(slot);

    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let id: u64 = (*process).id;
    let finalStatus: u64 = (*process).status;
    let killed: boolean = (*process).killedEnd;

    (*process).thread = Sched::Scheduler::currentId();
    (*process).state = (*process).detached ? FREE : EXITED;
    releaseChildren(id);
    wakeParent((*process).parent);
    Sched::Scheduler::setProcess(0);
    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    Drivers::Console::write("process ");
    Drivers::Console::writeDecimal(id);

    if (killed) {
      Drivers::Console::write(" killed, status ");
    } else {
      Drivers::Console::write(" exited with status ");
    }

    Drivers::Console::writeDecimal(finalStatus);
    Drivers::Console::writeLine("");

    Sched::Scheduler::exitCurrent();
  }

  /// Frees the children of `parent` that already ended, which nobody can
  /// wait for any more, and turns the ones still running into orphans that
  /// are freed when they end. The table lock is held.
  function releaseChildren(parent: u64): void {
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < MAX_PROCESSES) {
      let child: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

      if ((*child).state != FREE && (*child).parent == parent) {
        if ((*child).state == EXITED) {
          (*child).state = FREE;
        } else {
          (*child).parent = NO_PARENT;
          (*child).detached = true;
        }
      }

      slot += 1;
    }
  }

  /// Kills process `id`: it ends with `KILLED`, unless an earlier end set
  /// its status, as soon as each of its threads would run user code again.
  /// A thread waiting in a sleep, a wait or a keyboard read is woken so
  /// that it notices. Killing the calling process ends it at once (no
  /// return). Killing a process that already ended changes nothing.
  /// Returns `KILL_OK` or `KILL_NOT_FOUND`.
  function kill(id: u64): u64 {
    if (id == currentId()) {
      killCurrent(KILLED);
    }

    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let slot: u64 = findSlot(id);

    if (slot == NO_PROCESS) {
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      return KILL_NOT_FOUND;
    }

    let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

    if ((*process).state == RUNNING) {
      requestEnd(slot, KILLED, true);
    }

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return KILL_OK;
  }

  /// True when the running process was killed and has not ended yet.
  function killRequested(): boolean {
    let slot: u64 = currentSlot();

    return slot != NO_PROCESS && (*slotPointer(slot)).killPending;
  }

  /// Ends the calling thread when its process is ending; the status was
  /// recorded by the end that set `killPending`. The system call and
  /// interrupt paths call it right before they return to ring 3.
  function checkKill(): void {
    if (killRequested()) {
      finishThread(KILLED);
    }
  }

  /// Wakes the threads of process `parent` that are parked in `waitAny`, and
  /// leaves the others a token for their next park. Nothing happens for
  /// `NO_PARENT`. The table lock is held.
  function wakeParent(parent: u64): void {
    if (parent == NO_PARENT) {
      return;
    }

    let slot: u64 = findSlot(parent);

    if (slot != NO_PROCESS && (*slotPointer(slot)).state == RUNNING) {
      Sched::Scheduler::wakeProcess(slot + 1);
    }
  }

  /// Waits until any child of the running process has ended, writes its
  /// exit status to `status`, frees its slot and returns its id. Returns
  /// `NO_PROCESS` when the process has no child at all, or was killed. A
  /// child that ends between the scan and the park leaves a wake token, and
  /// the park returns at once.
  function waitAny(status: *mut u64): u64 {
    let me: u64 = currentId();

    while (true) {
      let mut found: boolean = false;
      let mut endedId: u64 = NO_PROCESS;
      let mut endedThread: u64 = 0;
      let mut endedSlot: u64 = 0;
      let mut slot: u64 = 0;

      Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

      while (slot < MAX_PROCESSES && endedId == NO_PROCESS) {
        let child: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

        if ((*child).state != FREE && (*child).parent == me) {
          found = true;

          if ((*child).state == EXITED) {
            endedId = (*child).id;
            endedThread = (*child).thread;
            endedSlot = slot;
          }
        }

        slot += 1;
      }

      Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

      if (endedId != NO_PROCESS) {
        // The child's last thread may still be dying: wait until it is gone
        // and its address space freed, as `wait` does. Only the parent collects
        // its children, so the slot still holds the child afterwards.
        Sched::Scheduler::join(endedThread);
        Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

        let child: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(endedSlot);

        if ((*child).state == EXITED && (*child).id == endedId) {
          *status = (*child).status;
          (*child).state = FREE;
          Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
          return endedId;
        }

        Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());
      } else if (!found || killRequested()) {
        return NO_PROCESS;
      } else {
        Sched::Scheduler::park();
      }

      if (killRequested()) {
        return NO_PROCESS;
      }
    }

    return NO_PROCESS;
  }

  /// The parent of process `id`: `NO_PARENT` for one the kernel started or
  /// an orphan, `NO_PROCESS` when no process has that id.
  function parentOf(id: u64): u64 {
    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let slot: u64 = findSlot(id);
    let parent: u64 = slot == NO_PROCESS ? NO_PROCESS : (*slotPointer(slot)).parent;

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return parent;
  }

  /// The slot of the process `id`, running or ended, or `NO_PROCESS`.
  function slotOf(id: u64): u64 {
    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let slot: u64 = findSlot(id);

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return slot;
  }

  /// The threads of process `id` that have not ended, 0 for an ended or
  /// unknown process.
  function threadCountOf(id: u64): u64 {
    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let slot: u64 = findSlot(id);
    let count: u64 = slot == NO_PROCESS || (*slotPointer(slot)).state != RUNNING ? 0 : (*slotPointer(slot)).threadCount;

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return count;
  }

  /// The state of process `id`: `RUNNING`, `EXITED`, or `FREE` when no slot
  /// holds it.
  function stateOf(id: u64): u64 {
    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let slot: u64 = findSlot(id);
    let state: u64 = slot == NO_PROCESS ? FREE : (*slotPointer(slot)).state;

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return state;
  }

  /// The number of slots in use, ended processes waiting for `wait`
  /// included. The self-tests check it to prove nothing is left behind.
  function usedSlots(): u64 {
    Sched::Mutex::lock(tableLock());

    let mut count: u64 = 0;
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < MAX_PROCESSES) {
      if ((*slotPointer(slot)).state != FREE) {
        count += 1;
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    Sched::Mutex::unlock(tableLock());

    return count;
  }

  /// Runs in the thread of a new process: drops to ring 3. The argument is
  /// the process slot.
  function userMain(slot: u64): void {
    let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

    // A process killed before it ever ran ends here, before any user code.
    checkKill();

    Arch::UserMode::enter((*process).entry, (*process).stackPointer);
  }

  /// The slot of the process whose thread is running, or `NO_PROCESS`: what
  /// `Sched::Scheduler::spawnMember` recorded, cleared again when the
  /// thread leaves the process. Read without a lock: only the thread itself
  /// clears it.
  function currentSlot(): u64 {
    let slotPlusOne: u64 = Sched::Scheduler::currentProcess();

    return slotPlusOne == 0 ? NO_PROCESS : slotPlusOne - 1;
  }

  /// `ProcessState::lock`.
  function tableLock(): *mut Sched::Mutex {
    return &mut ProcessState::lock as *mut Sched::Mutex;
  }

  /// The slot of the process with this id that is not free, or `NO_PROCESS`.
  /// The table lock is held.
  function findSlot(id: u64): u64 {
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < MAX_PROCESSES) {
      let process: *mut ProcessEntry = slotPointer(slot);

      if ((*process).state != FREE && (*process).id == id) {
        return slot;
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    return NO_PROCESS;
  }

  /// The first free slot, or `NO_PROCESS` when the table is full. The table
  /// lock is held.
  function findFreeSlot(): u64 {
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < MAX_PROCESSES) {
      if ((*slotPointer(slot)).state == FREE) {
        return slot;
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    return NO_PROCESS;
  }

  /// A pointer to the table entry of `slot`.
  function slotPointer(slot: u64): *mut ProcessEntry {
    return tableBase() + slot;
  }

  /// A pointer to the first entry of the process table.
  function tableBase(): *mut ProcessEntry {
    return &mut ProcessState::table as *mut ProcessEntry;
  }
}
